  approaches, noted once, not retried. The approaches below are mine alone.

  [1mThree approaches for the core[0m

  [1mA. Rule functions + per-field rule arrays[0m [3m(recommended)[0m
  A rule is just a function [38;5;153m(value, allValues) => string | null[39m — return an error message or null. A form spec maps
  field names to rule arrays:
  { [36musername[39m: [[33mrequired[39m(), [33mminLength[39m([32m3[39m)],
    [36mconfirm[39m:  [[33mrequired[39m(), [33mmatches[39m([31m"password"[39m, [31m"Passwords must match"[39m)] }
  Built-ins ship as factories ([38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m, [38;5;153memail[39m, [38;5;153mmatches[39m). Adding a rule means writing a
  function — no registration, no core change. Cross-field rules fall out for free because every rule sees [38;5;153mallValues[39m.
  [3mTrade-off:[0m the spec is JavaScript, so it can't be shipped as JSON from a server. Given the forms are hand-written
  pages here, that costs nothing.

  [1mB. Declarative spec object + named rule registry[0m
  Spec is pure data — [38;5;153m{ username: { required: true, minLength: 3 } }[39m — resolved against a registry of named validators,
  with [38;5;153mregisterRule(name, fn)[39m for custom ones.
  [3mBuys:[0m serializable specs, a spec you could store or generate.
  [3mCosts:[0m a layer of indirection between spec and behavior, a global mutable registry, custom error messages need a
  parallel mechanism, and cross-field rules need an escape hatch that breaks the data-only premise. Real cost for a
  benefit nothing here asks for.

  [1mC. Chainable schema builder[0m — [38;5;153mv.string().required().email()[39m, Zod-shaped.
  [3mBuys:[0m the nicest authoring ergonomics and self-documenting specs.
  [3mCosts:[0m by far the most machinery to build and maintain, and it's reimplementing a library badly. If you ever want
  this, installing Zod beats writing it.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m With the rules genuinely unknown, the cheapest extension point wins, and "write a function" is the
  cheapest one that exists. It's also the only option where cross-field validation isn't a special case. B and C both
  spend real complexity buying properties this project hasn't asked for — and A can grow into either later, since both
  could be built as a thin layer that emits rule functions.

  Does A look right before I detail the design?

